  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 941 PM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThe upper trough is exiting the area, with the Pacific cold front\nalso well to our east. Winds have diminished and shifted to the\nNNW as expected, with cooler, drier air filtering in. Some high\nclouds over southern Arizona will drift across northern sections\nof the CWA overnight, but will do little to affect temperatures. \nThe usual cold spots in the Gila have already dropped into the\n20s. In the lowlands, it's already 34 near Hachita, and 32 along\nUS-180 in NW Luna County. Some upper-30s are starting to pop up in\nSierra and Dona Ana County as well.\n\nThe Freeze Warning was expanded into southern Dona Ana County\nearlier this shift, after noting locally-generated bias-corrected\nNBM grids continued to show lows below freezing between Mesilla\nand La Union. Many of these areas managed to drop to the 33 to 35\nrange last night and this morning. Winds will be a little more \nactive tonight versus last night, but dewpoints will drop into the\n20s, with weak cold advection. This all favors a light freeze in\nthe lower elevations of the Mesilla Valley, but expect a quick\njump into the upper-30s with a little elevation rise.\n\nA slight wrinkle in the mid-term forecast, as some models and\nensemble members have trended deeper with the fast-moving upper\ntrough expected to pass through the area Monday night. The GFS\ngrazes northern Otero County with some light precipitation, while\nthe RRFS is much deeper and stronger, with precip (rain) as far\nsouth as El Paso late Monday night into Tuesday morning, and QPF\nsupportive of a 2-3 inches of snow in the Sacramento Mountains.\nThat seems a little extreme, and with the RRFS's short track \nrecord, a more measured approach is best. For now, 30 to 50 PoPs\nmainly for the Sacramento Mountains, and up to an inch of snow in\nthe grids, with snow levels down to about 7500 feet on the west\nslopes, and 5500 feet on the east slopes.",
